// arena-closure-reclaim probe (ADR 0002 phase 1a, RSS capstone): a CAPTURING
// closure's environment is an allocation site, so a non-escaping one is
// reclaimed by the enclosing arena.
//
// Read by scripts/arena_rss_probe.sh, which compiles this twice — once as
// written and once with `arena {` rewritten to `{` — and asserts the arena
// build's peak RSS is well below the leaking build's.
//
// SHAPE, and why it is not the obvious one. A captured variable becomes its
// own promoted heap slot (codegen_alloc_local_promoted), and those slots are
// NOT an arena site in this phase — they leak in BOTH builds. One closure per
// captured variable would therefore leak more than it reclaims and the RSS
// ratio would collapse toward 1.0, hiding a real regression. So: ONE captured
// variable, MANY environments over it. The inner loop allocates a fresh
// environment per pass from a single AST node, which keeps this one site for
// block_escape.c while making environments outnumber promoted slots 10:1.
//
// `double` never leaves its block — it is called in place, and the if-condition
// holding its result is a taint that walk_stmt computes and discards, never a
// sink. So the environment is provably block-local and 7c routes it to
// goo_arena_alloc. The escaping direction is covered separately, by probes that
// READ a closure after its arena closes.
//
// A miscompiled or prematurely freed environment reads garbage and panics
// before "done", so the golden/RSS harness fails loudly rather than quietly
// measuring the wrong thing.
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 100000; i++ {
		arena {
			v := i
			for j := 0; j < 10; j++ {
				double := func() int { return v * 2 }
				if double() != i*2 {
					panic("arena-closure-reclaim: mismatch")
				}
			}
		}
	}
	fmt.Println("done")
}
